<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valley Forge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1777">1777</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valley Forge was the winter encampment of the Continental Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, along the Schuylkill River, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. Unable to defend Philadelphia after his defeat at the Battle of Brandywine, Washington led his 12,000-man army into winter quarters there in December 1777, approximately 18 miles northwest of the city. The army faced a disastrous supply crisis, and an estimated 1,700 to 2,000 soldiers died from disease, possibly made worse by malnutrition and cold, wet weather. Baron Friedrich von Steuben, a Prussian drill master, instituted a training program before the army marched out in June 1778.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valley-forge.jpg" alt="Valley Forge" width="330" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Ward Dunsmore · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_and_Lafayette_at_Valley_Forge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1777 Valley Forge was a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> community at the meeting of Valley Creek and the Schuylkill River, founded around the Mount Joy Iron Forge that Quaker industrialists established in 1742. That summer the Continental Army's quartermaster general, Thomas Mifflin, placed a supply magazine there. On 18 September, after the British victories at the Battle of Brandywine and the Battle of the Clouds, Hessian soldiers under General Wilhelm von Knyphausen captured and burnt it. The site for winter quarters was chosen after Washington consulted his officers, with the army's march in on 19 December 1777; its high ground was easy to defend and open land allowed drilling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The army built its own shelter, the first large-scale construction of living quarters by the Continental Army: experts estimate between 1,500 and 2,000 log huts, each for a squad of twelve. About 12,000 people, soldiers, artificers, women and children, had to be supplied like a large colonial city, but the supply system had already broken down. Scabies, typhoid, dysentery, influenza, pneumonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a> spread, and most of the dead lay in general hospitals outside the camp. Washington ordered inoculation against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> after an outbreak among 3,000 to 4,000 men who had not been inoculated.</p><p>Between 250 and 400 women followed the army, earning money by washing clothes and nursing, and Martha Washington joined her husband in early February. The army was reorganised into five divisions, and Baron Friedrich von Steuben trained the troops in volley fire, march paces, skirmishing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> work. A group of officers and politicians, the so-called Conway Cabal, tried to replace Washington with Major General Horatio Gates, but it dissolved after leaks and exposures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The French alliance, signed on 6 February 1778, followed the surrender of John Burgoyne's army at Saratoga. The army marked the news with a Grand Review on 6 May. When the British under Clinton left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in June, the Continental Army pursued and fought at the Battle of Monmouth on 28 June, an inconclusive battle in which it performed better than before.</p><p>Valley Forge became an emblem of suffering and perseverance. The tale of a snowbound camp where men froze is not supported: snow fell rarely and temperatures were often above freezing, and the story probably comes from the colder winter of 1779 to 1780 at Jockey Hollow near Morristown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>. The site is now Valley Forge National Historical Park, set up in 1976.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valley Forge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>About 12,000 Continental soldiers marched into camp at <b>Valley Forge</b>, Pennsylvania.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-19">19 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
